# Step 3 — Create a Maven project

Let's create a very simple Java application.

Create a folder:

```bash
mkdir maven-demo
cd maven-demo
```

Create this folder structure:

```text
maven-demo
│
├── pom.xml
└── src
    └── main
        └── java
            └── com
                └── example
                    └── App.java
```

---

# Step 4 — Create `pom.xml`

Inside `maven-demo`, create:

```text
pom.xml
```

Put this code inside:

```xml
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-demo</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.source>17</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>17</maven.compiler.target>
    </properties>

</project>
```

### What is `pom.xml`?

`pom.xml` is the **Project Object Model** file. It contains information about the Maven project, dependencies, plugins, build configuration, and other project settings.

---

# Step 5 — Create the Java program

Create:

```text
src/main/java/com/example/App.java
```

Put this inside:

```java
package com.example;

public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello from Maven and Jenkins!");
    }
}
```

